>1. How do people continuously polymorph pets?

First you have to be resistant to polymorph. That requires magic resistance.
Think cloak of MR or grey dragon scale mail.

Now the polytrap continues to exist until you the player are ploymorphed 
by it. Well since you are resistant, the trap continues to exist even if
you sit right on top of it.

Now you can displace your pet into the trap polymorphing it. Now the only
problem left if when you get a pet form that cannot be further polymorphed.

I got a mimic once. What a waste!

Also note that your pets may volunteer to jump onto a polytrap. In my current
game my Doggie turned into a Black Dragon by choice.

> It seems to me that once
>they step on a polymorph trap, if they step on it again nothing will
>happen and I'm often left with a useless pet.

You just have to keep displacing it.

>2. How do people get so many hit points? The most I ever had was 350
>and it was the result of thousands of turns of potion collection and
>reverse nurse genociding. When I read YAAPs it seems standard to have
>over 400 and in some cases over 600.

All of those hit points are debatable. I've ascended with just a couple of
hundred before.

The item that you've probably missed is potion alchemy. You can elevate
healing potions all the way to full healing with each full healing giving
an extra 8 hit points.

Another strategy is to reverse geno nurses on a no teleport level. Then
they can't get away so fast.

Yet another is that you can get golden glows from praying. Each is worth
5 HP a pop. So pudding farmers can get hundreds of hit points (and
dozens of points of protection lowering their AC)

I've learned not to worry about HP as much as AC. When your AC is way negative
you're both hard to hit, and every hit only does fractional damage. Get your
AC down and lots of problems go away. Later in the game you can couple this 
with conflict. Sowhen you have a bunch of monsters around, they'll beat on 
one another instead of all of them beating on you.

>3. I find pets to be unbearably annoying. Whenever I've tried to use
>the pet as a bodyguard they end up running all over the place while I'm
>desperately running in circles around them trying to get them to attack
>whatever is chasing me until I die. How do other players use pets
>effectively?

Two words: magic whistle. Blow it and they teleport right to you.

The other task is to keep them from eating at critical time. See pets will
always eat when the opportunity arises. So when you have multiple attackers
you need to step on corpses to keep your pets from eating them.

